           </page>

           <page>

    <theorem>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>f: \R - \{1\} \to \R - \{0\}</m> be defined by
                <m>f(x) = \dfrac{1}{1-x}</m> for all <m>x \in \R - \{1\}</m>.
                Then <m>f</m> is a bijection.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>

    <proof>
        <p>
            Let <m>f: \R - \{1\} \to \R - \{0\}</m> be defined by
            <m>f(x) = \dfrac{1}{1-x}</m> for all <m>x \in \R - \{1\}</m>.
            We will prove that <m>f</m> is a bijection by proving that it is
            both a surjection and an injection.
        </p>

        <p>
            We will first show that <m>f</m> is a surjection.
            So, let <m>z \in \mathbb{R} - \{0\}</m> be any element of the
            codomain of <m>f</m>.
            Let <m>x = \frac{z-1}{z}</m>.
            We will show that <m>x \in \R - \{1\}</m> and that <m>f(x) = z</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
            We begin by showing that <m>x</m> is in the domain of <m>f</m>.
            To do this, we will show that <m>x \in \R</m> and <m>x \neq 1</m>.
            Real numbers are closed under subtraction and division (as long as
            they are nonzero), and by assumption <m>z \neq 0</m>, so
            <m>x \in \mathbb{R}</m>.
            Next we will show that <m>x \neq 1</m>.
            To see this, suppose that <m>x = 1</m>.
            Using algebra, we find that <m>1 = \frac{z-1}{z}</m>, which
            simplifies to <m>0 = -1</m>, a contradiction.
            Thus <m>x \neq 1</m>, and so <m>x \in \mathbb{R} - \{1\}</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
            Now we will show that <m>f(x) = z</m>.
            Using algebra,
            <md>
                <mrow> f\left(\frac{z-1}{z}\right)
                \amp= \dfrac{1}{1 - \left(\frac{z-1}{z}\right)}  </mrow>
                <mrow> \amp=
                \dfrac{1}{\frac{z}{z} - \frac{z-1}{z}} </mrow>
                <mrow> \amp= \dfrac{1}{\frac{z- (z-1)}{z}} </mrow>
                <mrow> \amp= \dfrac{1}{\frac{1}{z}} = z. </mrow>
            </md>
        </p>

        <p>
            Thus for each <m>z</m> in the codomain of <m>f</m>, there exists
            <m>x = \frac{z-1}{z}</m> in the domain of <m>f</m> such that
            <m>f(x) = z</m>.
            Therefore by definition, <m>f</m> is a surjection.
        </p>

        <p>
            We will now show that <m>f</m> is an injection.
            So, suppose that <m>f(x) = f(y)</m> for some
            <m>x, y \in \mathbb{R} - \{1\}</m>.
            We will show that <m>x = y</m>.
            Using algebra,
            <md>
                <mrow> f(x) \amp= f(y) </mrow>
                <mrow> \dfrac{1}{1-x} \amp= \dfrac{1}{1-y} </mrow>
                <mrow> 1-x \amp= 1-y  </mrow>
                <mrow> x \amp= y. </mrow>
            </md>
            Thus, for all <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> in the domain of <m>f</m>,
            whenever <m>f(x) = f(y)</m>, we must have <m>x = y</m>.
            Therefore <m>f</m> is an injection.
        </p>

        <p>
            Because we have shown that <m>f</m> is both a surjection and an
            injection, we have shown that <m>f</m> is a bijection.
        </p>
    </proof>

</page>
</handout>